"""Narrative produced only after the immutable primary and secondary results exist."""
from stage3c_setup import *
def fmt(s,digits=5):return f"mean {s['mean']:.{digits}f}, median {s['median']:.{digits}f}, SD {s['SD']:.{digits}f}, 95% equal-tail CrI [{s['CI95'][0]:.{digits}f}, {s['CI95'][1]:.{digits}f}]"
def main():
 if not read(E/'primary_replication_result.json')['scientifically_reportable']:
  from stage3c_failure_report import main as failure_report
  return failure_report()
 r=read(E/'primary_replication_result.json');full=read(E/'full_panel_result.json');p=read(E/'model_protocol.json');prior=read(E/'prior_predictive_summary.json');a=read(E/'primary_panel_model_data_summary.json');b=r['beta_RW'];diag=r['diagnostics'];v=r['variance_components'];con=r['probability_contrast'];c=con['conditional'];ppc=r['PPC'];fdiag=full['diagnostics'];source=read(ROOT/'docs/stage3b_evidence/part_b_authorization.json')
 negative=b['median']<0;direction='negative' if negative else 'nonnegative';cross=b['CI95'][0]<=0<=b['CI95'][1];comparison='the same negative direction as Stage 1/2' if negative else 'a different direction from the negative Stage 1/2 estimates';magnitude='smaller in absolute magnitude than both development estimates' if abs(b['median'])<.035309852 else 'larger in absolute magnitude than both development estimates' if abs(b['median'])>.037706514 else 'between the two development estimates in absolute magnitude'
 text=f'''# Stage 3C — Primary Multi-Season Hierarchical Replication\n\nScientific result and execution status are separate. The primary posterior median is **{direction}**; its 95% interval **{'includes' if cross else 'excludes'} zero**. Execution decision is recorded after verification in `stage3c_evidence/test_summary.json` and `execution_status.json`.\n\n## 1. Audit/provenance chain\n\nStage 0B froze eligibility and geometry; Stage 0C corroborated historical anthropometrics; Stages 1/2 developed the single-season association. Original Stage 3 remains BLOCKED under its MIN rule. Stage 3A remains UNRESOLVED regarding NBA internal minute precision. Stage 3B adopted a prospective variable-role protocol and released unchanged panel hashes before this first multi-season inference. All historical artifacts remain unchanged.\n\n## 2. Stage 3B authorization\n\n`part_b_authorized=true` was verified against the Stage 3B receipt and linked hashes before loading the primary model data. Primary SHA256: `{r['panel_hash']}`. Full secondary SHA256: `{full['panel_hash']}`. No panel was rebuilt or source data recollected. The pre-inference source-gate amendment remains an explicit part of this analysis's provenance, rather than a retrospective claim that the original gate passed.\n\n## 3. Scientific objective\n\nAssess whether the 2013–14 negative residual-wingspan association generalizes to other available seasons with repeated-player dependence, season adjustment and extra-binomial variation represented explicitly.\n\n{INTERPRETATION}\n\n## 4. Primary replication population\n\n{a['seasons']} seasons, {a['player_seasons']:,} player-seasons, {a['unique_players']} unique players and {a['FTA']:,} FTA. **2013–14 is excluded.** The likelihood uses {a['positive_FTA_rows']:,} positive-FTA rows; {a['zero_FTA_rows']} zero-FTA rows remain in descriptive accounting. There is no minimum-FTA threshold. The {a['zero_attempt_only_players']} players with no attempts anywhere in this panel remain in indexing/accounting; their random effects receive prior information only, not invented shooting observations. Player-season rows are repeated observations, not independent players.\n\n## 5. Frozen residual-wingspan exposure\n\nResidual wingspan = observed wingspan − (4.574910520685837 + 1.0013410045156863 × barefoot height), in inches. The Stage 1 transform is reused exactly, with no refitting or alternate exposure. The primary unique-player height mean, **{p['height_center']:.14f} inches**, was frozen for centering. Residual inches retain zero. Original position labels and hybrids are separate; missing/blank is unknown; C is reference. The earliest primary season, {p['season_reference']}, is reference.\n\n## 6. Hierarchical beta-binomial model\n\nFTM_it ~ BetaBinomial(FTA_it, mu_it × kappa, (1−mu_it) × kappa).\n\nlogit(mu_it) = beta_0 + beta_RW × residual_wingspan_i + beta_H × centered_height_i + position_effect_i + season_effect_t + u_i.\n\nNon-centered effects: z_i ~ Normal(0,1), u_i = sigma_player × z_i. kappa = exp(log_kappa), rho = 1/(1+kappa). There are no FTA, MIN, age, experience, team or other new predictors. FTA is only the count denominator.\n\n## 7. Identification interpretation\n\nResidual wingspan, height and combine position are time-invariant. **beta_RW is identified by between-player variation**, conditional on the hierarchical assumptions. Repeated seasons improve estimation of player propensity but do not create within-player variation in wingspan. Remaining random-intercept heterogeneity is assumed adequately represented by the specified distribution and its relation to the predictors. The random intercept does not control all player ability. Player fixed effects cannot separately estimate this time-invariant exposure and were not substituted.\n\n## 8. Priors\n\nFrozen before fitting at {p['frozen_at']}: beta_0 ~ Normal(0,2.5); beta_RW and beta_H ~ Normal(0,0.5); each nonreference original position and season coefficient ~ Normal(0,1); sigma_player ~ HalfNormal(1); log_kappa ~ Normal(3,2). No prior, exposure, covariate or formula changed after inspecting a scientific posterior. Computational amendments use spawned processes to avoid a recorded macOS fork crash and a validated PyTensor NUMBA compilation backend to avoid extremely slow uncompiled gradients. The PyMC NUTS sampler and statistical specification are unchanged.\n\n## 9. Prior predictive check\n\n1,000 prior draws: fraction mu<0.01 = {prior['fraction_below_001']:.4%}; mu>0.99 = {prior['fraction_above_099']:.4%}. All implied values are finite. Quantiles 2.5%, 25%, 50%, 75%, 97.5% are {', '.join(f"{prior['implied_mu_quantiles'][str(q)]:.6f}" for q in [.025,.25,.5,.75,.975])}.\n\nThese are deliberately broad priors, with appreciable near-boundary mass; this is not a realistic calibrated league-level prior distribution. They passed the predeclared gross-boundary screen (neither tail over 25%, no nonfinite values). The screen was frozen before the prior simulation, and no observed association was inspected to adjust it. Priors were retained as specified.\n\n## 10. MCMC diagnostics\n\nPyMC {r['software_environment']['packages']['pymc']}, four NUTS chains, {r['sampling']['tune']:,} tuning and {r['sampling']['draws']:,} retained draws per chain, target_accept={r['sampling']['target_accept']}, seed 20260928. Max rank-normalized R-hat **{diag['max_rhat']:.6f}**; minimum bulk ESS **{diag['min_bulk_ESS']:.1f}**, tail ESS **{diag['min_tail_ESS']:.1f}**; divergences **{diag['divergences']}**; maximum-tree-depth hits **{diag['max_treedepth_hits']}**; minimum BFMI **{min(diag['BFMI_by_chain']):.3f}**. Fixed-effect/variance posterior covariance is finite. All original position/season terms are included in the diagnostic gate, not just beta_RW. Trace and energy figures were visually reviewed before scientific summaries were released. Individual random effects are retained locally, not ranked or published.\n\nTwo initial process-launch attempts failed with EOFError and macOS fork-related segmentation faults, without producing a posterior trace. Both logs/receipts are retained. A third uncompiled run was interrupted for impractical measured gradient speed, with no completed posterior inspected. The successful run uses spawned workers and the supported [PyTensor NUMBA compilation mode](https://pytensor.readthedocs.io/en/latest/library/compile/mode.html), with reference-gradient agreement verified. It uses the same statistical model. Every completed or failed run and any diagnostic recovery are recorded in attempt artifacts; sampler success is not inferred from the association's sign.\n\n## 11. Primary beta_RW result\n\n**{fmt(b)}** log odds per additional residual-wingspan inch. P(beta_RW<0) = **{r['P_beta_RW_negative']:.6f}**. Direction is {direction}; the interval {'includes' if cross else 'excludes'} zero. Direction and posterior uncertainty are reported separately, without reducing the conclusion to a significance label.\n\n![Primary coefficient posterior](stage3c_figures/primary_01_beta_RW.png)\n\n## 12. OR interpretation\n\nOR per residual inch: **{fmt(r['OR_per_residual_inch'])}**. This summarizes conditional odds under the specified model; it is not a percentage-point difference, marginal population odds ratio, or causal effect.\n\n## 13. Q25/Q75 probability contrast\n\nQ25={con['Q25_inches']:.6f} and Q75={con['Q75_inches']:.6f} residual inches, calculated from the primary accounting player-season predictor distribution, with repeated seasons contributing their observed frequency. For each posterior draw, replace only residual wingspan, retain height, original position, season and that player's fitted random effect, then average equally over the {a['positive_FTA_rows']:,} positive-FTA rows.\n\nQ25 probability: {fmt(c['Q25'])}. Q75 probability: {fmt(c['Q75'])}. **Q75−Q25 percentage points: {fmt(c['difference_percentage_points'])}**, P(difference<0)={c['P_difference_negative']:.6f}.\n\nA secondary contrast sets all player effects to zero: difference {fmt(con['zero_random_effect_reference_player']['difference_percentage_points'])} percentage points. This is a **reference-player** contrast, not a population-average probability marginalized over the random-effect distribution. Both contrasts are adjusted model-based associations. Synthetic covariate combinations and extrapolation remain limitations.\n\n![Conditional contrast](stage3c_figures/primary_02_contrast.png)\n\n## 14. Player heterogeneity\n\nSigma_player: **{fmt(v['sigma_player'])}** on the logit scale. Around a hypothetical fixed baseline mu=0.75, −1 sigma gives median probability {v['probability_scale_at_reference_mu_075']['minus_one_sigma']['median']:.4f}, and +1 sigma gives {v['probability_scale_at_reference_mu_075']['plus_one_sigma']['median']:.4f}. This illustrates the distributional scale, not a ranking of actual players. No individual random-effect leaderboard is produced.\n\n## 15. Extra-binomial heterogeneity\n\nKappa: **{fmt(v['kappa'])}**. Rho: **{fmt(v['rho'])}**. Rho is additional player-season extra-binomial heterogeneity after the persistent random intercept; it is distinct from sigma_player. Stage 2 single-season rho≈0.0299 represented heterogeneity without longitudinal random intercepts. The values need not agree, because the persistent effect can absorb variation previously represented by single-season dispersion.\n\n## 16. Season effects\n\nSeason terms adjust the mean relative to {p['season_reference']}. Posterior summaries are saved in `primary_season_effects.csv`; no season-specific wingspan slope, season significance ranking or hypothesis search was performed.\n\n![Season adjustments](stage3c_figures/primary_03_seasons.png)\n\n## 17. Posterior predictive checks\n\n1,000 conditional beta-mixture/binomial replications preserve observed FTA and fitted player effects. Checks cover pooled makes/attempts; overall rate distribution, tails and boundary fractions; five FTA strata; season pooled rates; between-player pooled-rate SD; and mean within-player repeated-season rate SD. Observed makes={ppc['observed_total_FTM']:,}, attempts={ppc['FTA']:,}; observed pooled rate={ppc['pooled_rate']['observed']:.6f}, posterior predictive 95% range [{ppc['pooled_rate']['predictive_95_low']:.6f}, {ppc['pooled_rate']['predictive_95_high']:.6f}].\n\nMetrics outside their 99% predictive intervals: {', '.join(ppc['metrics_outside_99']) or 'none'}. Global-distribution flags={ppc['global_distribution_outside_99']}; season-rate flags={ppc['season_rates_outside_99']}. The frozen serious-failure rule did not trigger; visual findings are recorded verbatim in the PPC review artifact. These conditional checks are not independent out-of-sample prediction tests and do not establish complete model adequacy. Any localized misses remain documented rather than prompting coefficient-driven respecification.\n\n![Posterior predictive checks](stage3c_figures/primary_04_PPC.png)\n\n## 18. Comparison with Stage 1/2\n\nOnly after the primary result was frozen: the primary posterior has {comparison} and is {magnitude}. Stage 1 M2 beta≈−0.03531; Stage 2 beta-binomial≈−0.03771. Stage 3C median={b['median']:.6f}, mean={b['mean']:.6f}. Different populations, repeated-player structure, priors and dispersion models limit direct numerical equivalence. 2013–14 remains development evidence; overlapping players mean the replication is across seasons, not an independent-player replication. No agreement was forced.\n\n## 19. Secondary full-panel result\n\nFitted only after immutable primary freeze at {r['frozen_at']}. Full panel: 14 seasons, 4,428 player-seasons, 866 players, 511,686 FTA, including 2013–14. Same formula, priors, exposure and primary height center; a 2013–14 indicator is added to season coding. **This is not independent replication.**\n\nSecondary beta_RW: {fmt(full['beta_RW'])}; P(beta_RW<0)={full['P_beta_RW_negative']:.6f}. Max R-hat={fdiag['max_rhat']:.6f}; minimum bulk/tail ESS={fdiag['min_bulk_ESS']:.1f}/{fdiag['min_tail_ESS']:.1f}; divergences={fdiag['divergences']}. The secondary result does not replace the primary.\n\n## 20. Selection limitation\n\nReuse of approved Stage 3 coverage evidence gives approximately 51%–67% measurement coverage across seasons. This model does not correct selection into combine participation, available anthropometrics, NBA survival or shooting opportunity. Random intercepts do not solve selection. No new outcome-based measured/unmeasured comparison was calculated.\n\n## 21. Stage 3B minute-display limitation\n\n> {source['mandatory_limitation']}\n\n## 22. What the model does NOT establish\n\n{INTERPRETATION}\n\nNo within-player exposure identification, complete control of player ability, biomechanical mechanism or population-wide representativeness is established. Measurement error, frozen geometric-fit uncertainty, informative shooting opportunity, historical position, left-truncated NBA observation, common dispersion, omitted characteristics and the random-effects assumptions remain limitations.\n\n## 23. Reproducibility\n\nPython {r['software_environment']['python']}; exact isolated dependencies are in `requirements-stage3c.txt`, with successful pip check. The environment and traces are under ignored `models/stage3c/`; no previous environment changed. Protocol hash `{r['model_protocol_hash']}`. Trace path, size and SHA256 and code hashes are recorded in attempt receipts and the build manifest. Seeds and settings are frozen.\n\nRun scripts in this order: `stage3c_setup.py` once; `stage3c_model.py prior`; primary fit and diagnostics; record visual review; `stage3c_results.py analyze`; record PPC review; `stage3c_results.py freeze`; only then repeat fit/diagnostics/analysis/freeze for full. The setup and trace writers refuse accidental overwrite. macOS runs specify spawned workers and one BLAS thread. Two process-launch failures and the uncompiled interruption are retained separately; pinned Numba/LLVM dependencies and the computational amendments are recorded.\n\nNumerical summary replay from each saved trace is asserted exactly against the saved pending and frozen result. A separate primary sampler run repeats all frozen settings and seed in the same environment; every key-parameter mean must agree within four combined MCSEs. Its exact-array and tolerance results are recorded in `sampling_reproduction.json`. Neither check replaces or selects the frozen scientific result. Monte Carlo precision is separately assessed by four-chain mixing, ESS, R-hat and MCSE in saved diagnostic tables. Changes of library/platform can alter a seeded MCMC trajectory.\n\n## 24. Stage 3C execution decision\n\nThe final verifier records PASS only if hashes, accounting, model/protocol guards, prior checks, primary/secondary diagnostics, PPC review, primary-before-secondary order, exact saved-trace replay, the separate primary sampler reproduction and all tests pass. Scientific direction is not an execution criterion. Consult `execution_status.json` and `test_summary.json` for the executed decision.\n\n## 25. Recommended next step\n\nReview the primary hierarchical result and its between-player, selection and model-check limitations. Stage 3D sensitivities remain unexecuted and require separate authorization. No leave-one-season-out, FTA-threshold refits, grouped positions, alternate exposures, subgroups, season-varying slopes or new confounders were run. No commit or push was performed.\n'''
 (ROOT/'docs/stage3c_primary_hierarchical_replication.md').write_text(text)
if __name__=='__main__':main()
